1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,840
Léa: Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French!

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:04,980
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:06,980
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:10,280
Thank you for sharing a moment of your day with us.

5
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,680
Léa: Get comfortable.

6
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:15,540
Today, we're going to talk about our days,

7
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,940
from waking up until the evening.

8
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:21,440
Mathieu: I asked my colleagues to take part,

9
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,400
but they'd rather stay under the desk.

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:25,550
Léa: Your colleagues?

11
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,910
Mathieu: My slippers. They work from home with me.

12
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:33,340
Very discreet, always there, never late.

13
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:36,680
Léa: I hope you give them a lunch break.

14
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:40,720
Mathieu: Of course. They haven't filed a complaint yet.

15
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:44,640
Léa: This conversation is for levels B1 and B2.

16
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,580
We're going to speak slowly

17
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:49,320
and explain some useful expressions.

18
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:49,970
Mathieu: By the end,

19
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:53,420
you'll be able to describe your day in order

20
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:56,590
and talk about what you do regularly.

21
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:00,490
Léa: You'll also be able to explain what changes on certain days,

22
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:02,380
when your plans are a little different.

23
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:05,320
Mathieu: No need to have an extraordinary life.

24
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,900
Making breakfast or taking the bus,

25
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,120
that's already something to talk about.

26
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:13,760
Léa: And no need to understand every word.

27
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:15,020
Follow the stories,

28
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,680
then repeat a few sentences with us.

29
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:20,560
Mathieu: We're going to compare a day at the office

30
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:23,390
and a day working from home.

31
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,650
Léa: Then we'll help you

32
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:28,200
describe your own routine.

33
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:29,960
Mathieu: Even if it starts

34
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:34,000
with a long negotiation with your duvet.

35
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,920
We're ready to listen to you.

36
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:37,120
Léa: To start,

37
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:38,780
think of one small thing

38
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:41,440
you do almost every day.

39
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,920
Mathieu: Opening the windows, feeding your cat,

40
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:49,340
packing your bag or calling someone on your way home.

41
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:50,980
Léa: When do you do it?

42
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:53,750
And why do you like this habit?

43
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,120
Mathieu: Keep your idea for the end.

44
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:59,760
You can also tell us where you're listening to this episode from.

45
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,690
Léa: It's always nice to imagine

46
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:03,860
our conversations in your days,

47
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,360
at hours that are sometimes very different.

48
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:08,330
On the days I go to the office,

49
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:10,520
I wake up around seven.

50
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,880
I stay sitting for a little while before getting up.

51
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:16,430
Mathieu: Are you ready to chat straight away?

52
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,560
Léa: Not really. My head is still in a fog.

53
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,170
Mathieu: You have trouble thinking clearly,

54
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:25,600
as if your thoughts weren't quite awake yet.

55
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,980
Léa: Exactly. For example, after a long night journey,

56
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:32,160
my head was in a fog.

57
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,020
Mathieu: So I wait before asking you

58
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,680
to solve a complicated problem.

59
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:41,280
Léa: That's better. First, I open the shutters.

60
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:44,290
Then I make my breakfast.

61
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:46,720
I like to take my time at that moment.

62
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,100
Mathieu: To take your time

63
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:51,320
means doing something without rushing.

64
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:53,810
Léa: Yes. For example, on Saturdays,

65
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:56,880
I take my time choosing vegetables at the market.

66
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,030
Mathieu: But in the morning, you still keep an eye on the time?

67
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,560
Léa: A little. I eat, then I get ready.

68
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:04,410
I wash,

69
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:08,350
I get dressed and I check what I need to take.

70
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,930
Mathieu: "Se préparer," here,

71
00:03:09,930 --> 00:03:12,770
means doing the things you need

72
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:14,960
to be ready to leave.

73
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,050
Léa: That's it. Another example:

74
00:03:17,050 --> 00:03:19,920
we're getting ready for an evening at the theatre.

75
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,910
Mathieu: Do you make your lunch too?

76
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:25,240
Léa: Sometimes, but I often eat with my colleagues.

77
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:26,460
Before going out,

78
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:29,840
I grab my helmet and go downstairs to get my bike.

79
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:32,350
Mathieu: What time do you set off?

80
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,150
Léa: Around a quarter past eight.

81
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:35,390
"Se mettre en route"

82
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:38,360
means leaving, starting your journey.

83
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:42,290
Mathieu: For example, to get to my uncle's before noon,

84
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:45,280
we set off at ten.

85
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:48,600
Léa: On the way, I sometimes stop at the bakery.

86
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:52,280
I get some bread for lunch, then I head off again.

87
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:54,070
Mathieu: Do you meet any regulars?

88
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,650
Léa: Yes, including a man

89
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:58,960
who always orders before he's chosen.

90
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:02,310
The baker already knows how the conversation ends.

91
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:05,480
Mathieu: His purchase has become a little ceremony.

92
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:06,880
Léa: A bit.

93
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,260
My commute to the office takes about twenty minutes,

94
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:12,520
without that stop.

95
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:13,450
Mathieu: "Un trajet"

96
00:04:13,450 --> 00:04:15,140
is the journey

97
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:19,320
between a starting point and a destination.

98
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:21,410
Léa: Yes. For example,

99
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:23,700
her commute to the university

100
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:27,880
includes a ten-minute walk and a tram ride.

101
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:30,270
Mathieu: And when you get to the office?

102
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:34,520
Léa: I greet my colleagues and look at my appointments.

103
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:38,680
Around half past twelve, we often have lunch together.

104
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:40,040
Mathieu: So you can describe

105
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:43,160
several steps with very simple words.

106
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:47,560
Léa: Exactly: first, then, next.

107
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:51,560
They help the listener follow my day.

108
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:53,970
Mathieu: Me, when I work from home,

109
00:04:53,970 --> 00:04:56,910
I get up around half past seven.

110
00:04:56,960 --> 00:05:00,840
I make coffee and something to eat.

111
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:03,430
Léa: And your slippers start their day?

112
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:05,560
Mathieu: They're already there.

113
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:07,730
Then I water my plants

114
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:09,400
if they need it

115
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,680
and I air out the apartment a bit.

116
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:14,070
Léa: Do you start working right after?

117
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:17,130
Mathieu: Before, I'd drift from the table to the desk

118
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:20,520
without really deciding when to start.

119
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:23,670
Now, at nine o'clock, I get down to it.

120
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:24,630
Léa: "S'y mettre"

121
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:28,560
means starting an activity or a task.

122
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:32,360
Mathieu: Yes. For example, the boxes have arrived;

123
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,480
this afternoon, we're getting down to the tidying.

124
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:37,670
Léa: What helps you get started?

125
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:39,560
Mathieu: I move my mug,

126
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:44,240
I open my notebook and choose a first task.

127
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:47,240
These are very ordinary little actions.

128
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:51,190
Léa: But you know your work morning is starting.

129
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:52,760
Mathieu: That's it. Then

130
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:55,540
I can get caught up in a file

131
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,600
or a fairly long call.

132
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,880
Léa: "Être pris par quelque chose"

133
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,520
means being busy with that activity,

134
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:05,840
with little time for anything else.

135
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,930
Mathieu: For example, tomorrow morning,

136
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:11,120
I'll be busy preparing the meeting.

137
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:13,470
Léa: And what time does your lunch come?

138
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,080
Mathieu: Around one o'clock.

139
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:17,570
Sometimes I heat up a dish,

140
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:20,550
or I make something quick.

141
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:23,840
Then I go to another room to eat.

142
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:27,480
Léa: You take a break away from your work corner.

143
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:29,550
Mathieu: Yes. "Faire une pause"

144
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:33,240
means stopping an activity for a while,

145
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:35,000
before going back to it.

146
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,280
Léa: For example, during our visit to the museum,

147
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:40,800
we took a break in the garden.

148
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,040
Mathieu: In the afternoon, I go back to work.

149
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:46,920
I usually finish around six p.m.

150
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:49,040
and I put my things away.

151
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:50,270
Léa: And after that?

152
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:53,160
Mathieu: Sometimes I go and get a bit of shopping.

153
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:56,750
In the evening, I cook, I eat,

154
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:58,800
then I do the dishes.

155
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,910
Léa: Do you keep some time for yourself?

156
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:02,210
Mathieu: Yes. Often,

157
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:06,560
I listen to music or call a friend.

158
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,700
I go to bed around eleven p.m.,

159
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:10,920
but it depends on the day.

160
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:13,780
Léa: Exactly, to describe your routine,

161
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:15,560
you can say what happens

162
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:18,230
normally and what changes.

163
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:22,130
Mathieu: For example: "Usually, I work from home,

164
00:07:22,130 --> 00:07:24,640
but on Thursdays, I go to the office."

165
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:27,500
Léa: "D'habitude" means generally,

166
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,240
in ordinary circumstances.

167
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:34,800
Mathieu: Another example: usually we have lunch in the canteen,

168
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:38,040
but today, we're going to a restaurant.

169
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:42,000
Léa: Me, last Wednesday, it was raining a lot.

170
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,950
I left my bike at home

171
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,640
and took the tram.

172
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:47,350
Mathieu: Did you have to leave earlier?

173
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:50,680
Léa: Yes, and get my bread near the office.

174
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,770
The steps were almost the same,

175
00:07:52,770 --> 00:07:55,120
but their order had changed.

176
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:58,350
Mathieu: You didn't need to rebuild your whole day.

177
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:01,090
Léa: No. These little landmarks help me,

178
00:08:01,090 --> 00:08:04,240
even when my schedule shifts.

179
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,640
Mathieu: And our energy changes too.

180
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:09,920
Some afternoons, I hit a slump.

181
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:12,440
Léa: It's a sudden drop in energy.

182
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:16,390
You feel tired and want to slow down.

183
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:20,160
Mathieu: For example, after carrying all those boxes,

184
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:21,640
I suddenly felt exhausted

185
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:23,360
and sat down.

186
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:25,240
Léa: You can then explain:

187
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:28,080
"Around three p.m., I'm sometimes tired.

188
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:30,120
I stop for a few minutes."

189
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:32,610
Mathieu: That's closer to my real life than saying

190
00:08:32,610 --> 00:08:34,220
that I always work

191
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:36,440
enthusiastically until dinner.

192
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:38,980
Léa: And for someone who works in the evening,

193
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:41,000
or who looks after a loved one,

194
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,600
the hours will be different again.

195
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:47,160
Mathieu: Yes. Your routine tells your story.

196
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:50,560
It doesn't need to look like ours.

197
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:52,650
Léa: Even slightly messy days

198
00:08:52,650 --> 00:08:55,000
give you things to talk about.

199
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,880
Mathieu: Let's try a scene.

200
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:00,810
We meet before our first pottery class

201
00:09:00,810 --> 00:09:03,600
and we chat about our days.

202
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:07,680
Léa: You can answer my question before Mathieu.

203
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:08,720
Mathieu: For once,

204
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:12,390
I'm not going to start by talking about my slippers.

205
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:17,070
Léa: So, what does a typical day look like for you?

206
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:18,150
Mathieu: Your turn!

207
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:22,760
Describe the start of your day in two sentences.

208
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:25,360
Take a few seconds.

209
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,400
Léa: You can start with:

210
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:30,390
"On weekdays, I get up around…

211
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,670
Then I…"

212
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:36,320
Mathieu: On weekdays, I get up around half past seven.

213
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:39,360
Then I have breakfast

214
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:41,630
and I work from home.

215
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:43,470
Léa: What time do you start?

216
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:45,320
Mathieu: Around nine.

217
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:48,910
And you, do you work from home too?

218
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:50,490
Léa: Some days, yes.

219
00:09:50,490 --> 00:09:53,120
But I often cycle to the office.

220
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:54,040
On the way home,

221
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:57,400
I sometimes take a detour to stop by the market.

222
00:09:57,400 --> 00:09:58,830
Mathieu: "Faire un détour"

223
00:09:58,830 --> 00:10:01,600
means taking a longer route

224
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:05,120
or one that differs from the direct route.

225
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:06,060
Léa: For example,

226
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:08,400
we took a detour to see

227
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:10,670
the old bridge before going home.

228
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:13,350
Mathieu: OK. And do you have time

229
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:16,110
to cook before the class?

230
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:20,120
Léa: Not always. On Wednesdays, I often eat afterwards.

231
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:23,520
I make something simple before leaving.

232
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:24,760
Mathieu: Me too.

233
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:29,320
I like having my dinner almost ready when I get home.

234
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:32,150
Léa: And what do you do on the other evenings?

235
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:34,150
Mathieu: I often stay at home,

236
00:10:34,150 --> 00:10:38,670
but on Fridays, I sometimes see friends.

237
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:41,440
Léa: There's a conversation that can keep going.

238
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:44,300
You ask a question, you answer,

239
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:47,120
then you take an interest in the other person.

240
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:49,440
Mathieu: You don't need to describe

241
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:51,920
twenty-four hours without stopping.

242
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:55,480
A few steps are enough to start.

243
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:59,520
Léa: To organise your story, choose three moments:

244
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:03,590
the morning, during the day and the evening.

245
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:07,940
Mathieu: For each moment, you can give an action,

246
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:11,320
a time and a small personal detail.

247
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:15,440
Léa: For example: "In the morning, I leave around eight.

248
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:19,040
I like to walk a little before taking the tram."

249
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,130
Mathieu: Or: "After lunch,

250
00:11:21,130 --> 00:11:24,760
I go out to stretch my legs."

251
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:26,350
Léa: "Se dégourdir les jambes"

252
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:28,200
means walking a little after

253
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:31,270
sitting or staying still.

254
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:35,170
Mathieu: For example, on a long car journey,

255
00:11:35,170 --> 00:11:38,640
we stop to stretch our legs.

256
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:39,330
Léa: You see,

257
00:11:39,330 --> 00:11:43,600
that little detail already paints a picture of your afternoon.

258
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:48,210
Mathieu: To talk about frequency, I can say "often,"

259
00:11:48,210 --> 00:11:51,760
"sometimes" or "almost every day."

260
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:54,640
Léa: Yes. "I cook almost every day"

261
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:56,850
and "I sometimes cook"

262
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:59,750
don't describe the same habit.

263
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:00,630
Mathieu: And in the evening,

264
00:12:00,630 --> 00:12:04,720
I can explain why I do an activity.

265
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:08,880
"I listen to music to take my mind off things."

266
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:12,320
Léa: "Se changer les idées" means thinking about something else,

267
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:15,880
often thanks to a pleasant activity.

268
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:19,810
Mathieu: Another example: after that long meeting,

269
00:12:19,810 --> 00:12:23,720
I went to the cinema to take my mind off things.

270
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:26,830
Léa: You can also talk about a particular day:

271
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:27,620
"On Tuesdays,

272
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:31,240
I get home later because I have a class."

273
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:35,630
Mathieu: And what if my routine is still changing?

274
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:37,080
Léa: You can say so.

275
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:37,930
"At the moment,

276
00:12:37,930 --> 00:12:40,840
I'm trying to make my lunch the night before."

277
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:43,760
That describes a new habit.

278
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:47,040
Mathieu: I'm not going to transform my whole day

279
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:48,760
overnight.

280
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:50,280
Léa: "Du jour au lendemain"

281
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:54,310
means very quickly, without a long transition.

282
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:56,610
Example: he can't change

283
00:12:56,610 --> 00:12:59,720
all his hours overnight.

284
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:04,200
Mathieu: So I start with a sentence that suits me.

285
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:08,800
Then I add a detail when I feel comfortable.

286
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:11,200
Léa: Exactly. You can learn

287
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:13,720
to describe your day gradually,

288
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:15,800
with the vocabulary you already know.

289
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:18,720
Mathieu: And ask for a word when it's missing.

290
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:21,950
"How do you say…?" is still a very useful question.

291
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,350
Léa: It's your turn.

292
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:29,720
Here's a model: "Usually, I get up around…

293
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:34,240
First, I… Then, I…

294
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:36,480
In the evening, I…"

295
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:39,960
Mathieu: Usually, I get up around half past seven.

296
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:42,920
First, I make breakfast.

297
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:45,120
Then I work.

298
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:47,280
In the evening, I cook.

299
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:49,430
Léa: I can add:

300
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:51,960
"I often cycle to the office.

301
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:55,920
But when it rains a lot, I take the tram."

302
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:59,840
Mathieu: Say your version out loud.

303
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:02,680
Take time to find the words.

304
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:05,320
You can use short sentences.

305
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:09,960
Léa: Now add your little habit from the beginning:

306
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:12,680
"Almost every day, I…

307
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:14,080
because…"

308
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:15,680
Mathieu: Almost every day,

309
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:18,150
I air out my apartment in the morning

310
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:20,210
because I like to start the day

311
00:14:20,210 --> 00:14:21,680
with fresh air.

312
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:23,160
Léa: Almost every day,

313
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:26,320
I chat with a colleague during lunch

314
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:29,120
because I enjoy that moment together.

315
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:33,480
Mathieu: And to keep the conversation going, ask a question:

316
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:36,430
"And you, what do you do after work?"

317
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:39,680
Léa: Let's go back over five expressions.

318
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:42,640
Listen, then repeat with us.

319
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:47,790
Mathieu: First expression: "avoir la tête dans le brouillard."

320
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:48,610
When she wakes up,

321
00:14:48,610 --> 00:14:52,480
Léa isn't always ready to think very fast.

322
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:55,560
Léa: It means your thoughts aren't very clear.

323
00:14:55,560 --> 00:14:58,080
New example: this morning,

324
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:00,320
my head was in a fog

325
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:03,520
and I reread the same message three times.

326
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:08,480
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "avoir la tête dans le brouillard."

327
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:11,340
An expression to describe that feeling

328
00:15:11,340 --> 00:15:13,440
without recounting your whole night.

329
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:17,430
Léa: Second expression: "se mettre en route."

330
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,490
I leave around a quarter past eight

331
00:15:19,490 --> 00:15:21,000
to go to the office.

332
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,600
Mathieu: It means starting your journey.

333
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:26,680
Example: the concert starts soon,

334
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:29,120
it's time for us to set off.

335
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:32,840
Léa: Let's repeat: "se mettre en route."

336
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:36,360
You can specify the time or the destination.

337
00:15:36,360 --> 00:15:39,880
Mathieu: Third expression: "s'y mettre."

338
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:42,840
At nine o'clock, I start my work.

339
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:45,760
Léa: You start the activity you're talking about.

340
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:48,720
Example: to prepare the show,

341
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:51,560
we're getting down to it from Saturday.

342
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:54,320
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "s'y mettre."

343
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:56,920
In a conversation, you often hear:

344
00:15:56,920 --> 00:15:58,990
"Right, shall we get started?"

345
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:02,880
Léa: Fourth expression: "un coup de barre."

346
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:06,800
Some afternoons, you feel a drop in energy.

347
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:09,880
Mathieu: It's a moment of sudden tiredness.

348
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:12,400
Example: after the visit,

349
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:14,060
she suddenly felt exhausted

350
00:16:14,060 --> 00:16:15,910
and preferred to go home.

351
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:18,720
Léa: Let's repeat: "un coup de barre."

352
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,550
It's a common expression

353
00:16:20,550 --> 00:16:23,000
in everyday conversations.

354
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,760
Mathieu: Finally, "se changer les idées."

355
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:30,120
In the evening, music helps me think about something else.

356
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:33,640
Léa: Example: we went to see an exhibition

357
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:37,360
to take our minds off things after a busy week.

358
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:40,710
Mathieu: Let's repeat: "se changer les idées."

359
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:44,040
You can explain which activity you enjoy

360
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:46,360
and when you do it.

361
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:50,290
Léa: Now choose an expression and make a sentence

362
00:16:50,290 --> 00:16:54,280
about your day yesterday or your usual routine.

363
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:57,200
Mathieu: Little details are welcome.

364
00:16:57,200 --> 00:16:58,880
It's often thanks to them

365
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:02,040
that a conversation becomes personal.

366
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:05,200
Léa: In the comments, complete this sentence:

367
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:08,280
"Almost every day, I…

368
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:09,470
because…"

369
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:11,390
Mathieu: Or describe your day

370
00:17:11,390 --> 00:17:14,610
in three steps with "first,"

371
00:17:14,660 --> 00:17:17,190
"then" and "finally."

372
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:19,160
Léa: If this episode helped you,

373
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:23,040
subscribe to The Cozy French to keep

374
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:25,640
listening and speaking with us.

375
00:17:25,640 --> 00:17:28,650
Mathieu: You can listen to a short exchange again,

376
00:17:28,650 --> 00:17:32,920
then try to retell it with your own habits.

377
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:35,200
Léa: Thank you for sharing this moment.

378
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:38,150
Take care and see you very soon!

379
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,510
Mathieu: Goodbye everyone!

380
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,800
Now I'm going to announce the end

381
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:44,000
of the day to my colleagues.

382
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,950
Léa: So your slippers can finally go home?

383
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:48,560
Mathieu: They're already there.

384
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:49,870
But for tonight,

385
00:17:49,870 --> 00:17:53,400
they're leaving the office and joining the sofa.

386
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:56,140
Léa: A very reasonable commute.
